  "findings": [
    "The new measurements distinguish elapsed file-sync and directory-sync calls inside each service publication. Both types can contribute materially to the observed delays; one publication type cannot be assumed to explain every tail.",
    "Call counts identify aggregation. Automatic archive work is included in journal_begin; a count of one distinguishes a single measured barrier from repeated archive syncs.",
    "The storage representation, file/rename/directory order, synchronous durability barriers, failure poisoning, replay checks and completion semantics are unchanged. Older policy readers can still read the same JSON journal and archives."
  ],
  "limits": [
    "Sync elapsed time brackets the runtime fs.fsyncSync call and includes binding overhead and descheduling, not just kernel execution or storage device service time. This does not establish a flash, writeback, CPU-pressure or kernel-lock cause.",
    "Nested sync intervals are contained by journal_begin/journal_finish. Adding both levels would double-count time. Failed sync attempts are counted but do not imply a durable publication.",
    "Four fixed public admissions and forgets per implementation/device are bounded mixed root/app samples, not stable tail distributions. Earlier direct-worker and public-path observations remain separate evidence; performance no-regression is not yet proved.",
    "The independent observer still costs CPU/memory and uses periodic resource samples with sparse mapping detail. The capture is not exact process-tree peak or 24-hour endurance evidence.",
    "All 19 payload members, including their manifests and the managed bridge/worker fallback, are unchanged from 0.7.47. Independent .NET SDK rescue remains available. No reboot/standby, global policy, persistent file capability or SMACK change occurred.",
    "Native-only entry remains unqualified. Source review of the existing vendor-entry evidence did not establish a new usable entry route; no new loader/daemon was executed in that investigation."
  ]
}
